---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description } from '../../content';

const path = '/guides/tcpa';
const title = 'TCPA Compliance for Shopify SMS';
const desc = description(site, 'guides/tcpa');


---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <a href="/guides" class="link">← All SMS guides</a>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p>For a Shopify marketing team, a TCPA review should produce a traceable answer to a specific question: why was this sender allowed to send this message to this number at this time? This US-focused guide organizes that review. It cannot determine your program's legal status or cover every state and international rule.</p>
    <h2>Separate the legal questions from supplier policy</h2>
    <p><a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-47/chapter-I/subchapter-B/part-64/subpart-L/section-64.1200">47 CFR 64.1200(a)(2) and (f)(9)</a> address prior express written consent for covered automated telemarketing and its signed agreement, seller, number, authorization and no-purchase-condition elements. Applicability depends on the technology and facts. Paragraphs (c)–(e) separately address solicitation and do-not-call rules; an autodialer argument does not resolve every obligation.</p>
    <p>State law also needs review: <a href="https://www.leg.state.fl.us/statutes/index.cfm?App_mode=Display_Statute&amp;URL=0500-0599/0501/Sections/0501.059.html">Florida section 501.059</a>, for example, expressly includes texts in its telephonic-sales-call definition. This is a jurisdiction example, not a fifty-state checklist.</p>
    <p><a href="https://www.twilio.com/en-us/legal/messaging-policy">Twilio's policy</a> requires written consent for promotional messaging, limits consent by subject and distinguishes direct conversational replies from recurring engagement. These are Twilio service conditions, not a statement that every legal rule uses the same scope.</p>
    <h2>Classify content before choosing a consent path</h2>
    <p>Review the actual message and destination page, not just the flow name. A shipping update that adds a discount is different from a bare shipment notice. A cart reminder seeks a purchase; calling it a service message does not settle its classification. Keep the approved template version alongside its purpose and permitted audience.</p>
    <p>Ask the legal reviewer to map each purpose, sender, recipient jurisdiction and sending technology to the relevant requirements. Record unresolved cases as holds. A customer entering a number for delivery is not evidence that they agreed to recurring promotions. A purchase, VIP tag or email subscription should not become the SMS eligibility field.</p>
    <h2>Create an evidence packet that survives migration</h2>
    <p>For each capture surface, preserve the rendered disclosure and form version, named sender, promised message scope, telephone number, timestamp and affirmative action. Keep the terms and privacy versions shown at that time, plus later withdrawals and changes. A current screenshot cannot demonstrate what a subscriber saw last year.</p>
    <p>Link the packet to a durable consent event rather than overwriting one Boolean on the customer profile. Preserve the source system and import lineage when moving providers. Quarantine rows whose permission cannot be demonstrated; exporting a list does not repair missing evidence. Set retention and access controls with the reviewer rather than inventing one universal retention period.</p>
    <p>Review checkout, popup, keyword, support and imported records separately. A keyword campaign needs its associated promotion and disclosure, not just the keyword string. A support conversation needs its own purpose boundary. Merchant-specific wording should be approved in context; a copied disclosure alone cannot guarantee a valid program.</p>
    <h2>Make revocation a dispatch control</h2>
    <p>The federal rule recognizes reasonable revocation methods, including intelligible non-keyword replies. Build a prompt suppression path and human escalation for ambiguous requests; do not require only an exact STOP match. Review current rule timing and scope with counsel before launch.</p>
    <p>Operationally, record the request before closing the support ticket, propagate it to every relevant sender and recheck queued campaigns. Prevent a stale import from reactivating the profile. Keep marketing suppression separate from ticket status: resolving an order issue does not itself restore marketing permission. A confirmation should not become a new sales opportunity.</p>
    <h2>A proposed audit from message back to permission</h2>
    <p>No audit or device test was performed for this guide. For a controlled sample from each active flow, trace the outgoing template to its purpose, enrollment event, disclosure version, consent evidence, jurisdiction review, send-window decision and latest suppression state. Include a migrated contact and a contact whose permission changed while queued.</p>
    <p>Propose negative cases as well: missing disclosure version, email-only signup, opt-out through ordinary language, duplicate customer profiles and an old import after withdrawal. The expected program behavior is a documented hold or suppression where eligibility is absent, with a named owner for resolving mismatches. Inspect provider logs and merchant records together; a vendor checkbox cannot prove that the form, integration and queue all agree.</p>
    <h2>Choose tooling by the evidence it can expose</h2>
    <p>Ask shortlisted suppliers to demonstrate exportable consent history, suppression propagation, inbound escalation and queue cancellation for your setup. Distinguish built-in controls from work your team must integrate. Sender registration or carrier approval does not replace subscriber permission or jurisdiction review.</p>
    <p>Continue with <a href="/guides/consent">consent capture</a>, <a href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet-hour scheduling</a> and <a href="/guides/two-way-sms">reply operations</a>. The <a href="/apps">app catalog</a> supports discovery; no app listed here is a guarantee of compliance. Revisit the review when forms, countries, message purposes or providers change.</p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
